-- EXPECT-ERROR: cap verified
--
-- `cap verified` escalates an undischarged POSTCONDITION, not just an
-- undischarged precondition (Task 3 of the 2026-07-30 refinement backlog).
-- Until this fix, a return refinement the checker could neither prove nor
-- refute was silently SKIPPED, even inside `cap verified` -- the one mode
-- whose entire premise is "if it compiles, it is proved". A `cap verified`
-- module with an undischargeable return contract exited 0.
--
-- `mk` declares `{Int | _ > 0}` as its return type over a body the checker
-- cannot decide: `z * z + 1` is nonlinear, outside the reflectable fragment,
-- and TRUE on every input so the witness battery finds no counterexample
-- either (2026-08-30: the original body `z` became a witness-CONFIRMED
-- violation -- a different, stronger error that no longer mentions `cap
-- verified`). The obligation is therefore SKIPPED
-- -- and under `cap verified` that skip is now an error,
-- exactly as an undischarged precondition already was (see
-- `check_call`'s escalation, which this mirrors for the return position).
--
-- Companion accept witness `accept/t135_refine_postcondition_strict_trusted`
-- is the same module with `@[trusted]` added to `mk`, which rescues the skip
-- instead of escalating it.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md.
mod T134 do

  cap verified

  fn mk(z : Int) : {Int | _ > 0} do
    z * z + 1
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    mk(1)
  end

end
